  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA small window of drier conditions should unfold for the next 6 to 9 \nhours for many areas in the wake of the departing MCV and associated \nprecip. Upstream across IL a few showers are developing and can't \nrule out widely scattered showers and storms later today into this \nevening in areas that see limited clearing (mainly SE). SPC has \nmaintained the Marginal Risk for severe in our SE with a low chance \nfor some locally gusty winds from any more organized storms that \nproduce wet microbursts, much like yesterday. \n\nThe greater concern lies with flooding potential later tonight into \nThursday. Most areas across N Indiana and to a lesser extent NW Ohio \nhave experienced multiple rounds of showers and storms and all signs \npoint towards another round overnight developing upstream and moving \nESE along the quasi stationary boundary that will be draped across \nthe area. PWATs continue around 2\" and probably even higher with no \nmajor change in air mass expected until later Thursday into Friday. \nWPC has all but out MI counties in a Slight Risk for excessive \nrainfall, which is very reasonable given the setup. While confidence \nis somewhat higher on at least parts of the area being impacted, \nsome CAMs (especially the HRRR) develop 2 rounds with one shortly \nafter 6Z across southern areas and then the upstream development \narriving towards 12Z Thu. While a Flood Watch could be easily \njustified given the wet conditions and potential for another 1 to as \nmuch as 3 inches in some spots, confidence on timing and location of \nthe heaviest precip remains low enough to hold off on any headlines. \nPops have been adjusted to reflect trends with a larger, \nbroadbrushed likely area late tonight into Thursday morning with \nprecip then ending from west to east. \n\nLate Thursday into Friday it still appears that much, if not all of \nthe area should see a breather in rain chances along with somewhat \nlower humidity. Late Friday night into Sat this changes as yet \nanother wave moves towards the region which forces the front back \nnorth and brings rain chances with it. Hydro concerns will likely \nremain given the small window of drying.",
